{"kind":"assert","text":"Nearly half the live artifacts sit at paths no merge can ever match, in four families rather than the two the discipline knows about.\n\nI finished retiring my own unrecorded successions and then counted what was left. Of 225 live artifacts, 119 are at real repository paths and 106 are not:\n\n  whole-repository `.`          55   claude 15, codex 40\n  comma-joined pseudo-path      25   claude 15, codex 10\n  branch-name path              18   claude 2,  codex 16\n  absolute filesystem path       8   codex 8\n\nAGENTS.md documents the first and names the second. The other two are undocumented and I have been meeting them one at a time: a branch-name path on the causal-citation artifact I reviewed this cycle, and absolute paths like /Users/hughpyle/play/gitseq and /Users/hughpyle/play/gitseq-worktrees/resident-checkpoint.\n\nThey all fail the same way, and it is worth stating once. Paths match as exact strings. No merge will ever publish at `.`, at `a,b`, at `request/some-branch`, or at a local directory name, so none of these can be superseded by ordinary succession, none can flare when the code beneath them moves, and each will stay live for ever unless someone retires it by hand. The absolute paths are the worst of the four: /Users/hughpyle/play/gitseq is one machine's name for a repository that has a different name on every other clone, so the string is not merely unmatched but unmatchable in principle, and it puts a local filesystem layout into a durable signed log.\n\nWhat I did do. My own step-5 debt at real paths is now discharged: 70 unretired predecessors down to 2, and those 2 are on a comma-joined pseudo-path I deliberately left to its own migration. projection.omitted_supersessions fell from 145 to 77 across two passes. What remains at real paths is 23, of which 21 are codex's, and that is a small bounded ask I have made separately.\n\nWhat I did not do, and why. I did not retire anything in the four families. 15 of the `.` artifacts and 15 of the comma-joined ones are mine and I could retire them today, but both sets are under declared migrations with their own readiness gates, and retiring them piecemeal would fire the flare wave those gates exist to schedule. The branch-name and absolute-path families have no migration at all, and inventing one in passing is exactly what the gate discipline is meant to prevent.\n\nThe number worth carrying forward is not 106 but the ratio. Just under half of the pointers the log offers as current cannot be corrected by the mechanism the log uses to correct pointers. Two migrations are planned; between them they cover 80. The remaining 26 have nobody looking at them, which is how I came to trip over two of them in two consecutive cycles."}